Okay, so I need to figure out the sum of all prime numbers p where there's no integer solution x to the congruence 3(6x + 1) ≡ 4 mod p. Hmm, let me start by understanding what this congruence means and how to approach it.

First, maybe I should simplify the congruence. Let me expand the left side: 3*(6x + 1) is 18x + 3. So the equation becomes 18x + 3 ≡ 4 mod p. If I subtract 3 from both sides, that gives 18x ≡ 1 mod p. So the problem is equivalent to finding primes p for which the equation 18x ≡ 1 mod p has no solution. 

Wait, but in modular arithmetic, a linear congruence like ax ≡ b mod p has a solution if and only if a and p are coprime, right? Because if they are coprime, then a has a multiplicative inverse modulo p, so you can solve for x. But if a and p are not coprime, then the equation has a solution only if b is divisible by the greatest common divisor
